{"id":"rel.wave2.batch05.unit-of-ring.to.associative-ring-identity-optional","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.unit-of-ring","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.associative-ring-identity-optional","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"associative ring","editorial_reason":"A multiplicatively invertible element is defined inside an associative ring with identity. The underlying ring axioms are prerequisites; the unit and two-sided inverse conditions are additional.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AUnit_of_Ring","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ARing_%28Abstract_Algebra%29","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_algebra_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch05.integral-domain.to.associative-ring-identity-optional","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.integral-domain","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.associative-ring-identity-optional","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"commutative ring","editorial_reason":"A nonzero integral domain requires commutative unital ring multiplication. The independently sourced associative ring supplies underlying operations, while commutativity, unity and nonzero hypotheses remain explicit.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09FE","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ARing_%28Abstract_Algebra%29","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_algebra_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch05.integral-domain.to.zerodivisor-of-ring","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.integral-domain","target_meaning":"meaning.559efcb6f15ddc96.zerodivisor-of-ring","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"ab=0","editorial_reason":"The Stacks definition characterizes domains by absence of nonzero zerodivisors, equivalently the stated zero-product criterion. This link uses the separately screened zerodivisor definition in the same commutative ring context.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09FE","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02LV","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_algebra_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch05.commutative-maximal-ideal.to.two-sided-ideal","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.commutative-maximal-ideal","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.two-sided-ideal","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"An ideal","editorial_reason":"A maximal ideal is first an ordinary ring ideal and then a proper ideal maximal under inclusion. The general two-sided ideal meaning supplies the underlying algebraic structure.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AMaximal_Ideal_of_Ring","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AIdeal_of_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_algebra_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch05.commutative-maximal-ideal.to.associative-ring-identity-optional","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.commutative-maximal-ideal","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.associative-ring-identity-optional","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"commutative ring","editorial_reason":"A maximal ideal of a commutative unital ring is defined inside a ring. The general associative ring provides underlying addition and multiplication without erasing extra commutativity or unity hypotheses.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AMaximal_Ideal_of_Ring","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ARing_%28Abstract_Algebra%29","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_algebra_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch05.associate-elements.to.unit-of-ring","from_formulation":"form.05531f966e3a206f.associate-elements","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.unit-of-ring","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"invertible element","editorial_reason":"Associated elements are related by multiplication by an invertible ring element. The unit-of-unital-ring meaning matches precisely that prerequisite in an integral domain.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/034O","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AUnit_of_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_algebra_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch05.associate-elements.to.integral-domain","from_formulation":"form.05531f966e3a206f.associate-elements","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.integral-domain","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"integral domain","editorial_reason":"The associate relation is formulated for elements of an integral domain; the newly screened nonzero commutative-unital domain meaning supplies that context without defining associatedness itself.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/034O","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09FE","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_algebra_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch05.irreducible-element.to.unit-of-ring","from_formulation":"form.05531f966e3a206f.irreducible-element","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.unit-of-ring","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"unit","editorial_reason":"Irreducibility is specified by nonunit status and a unit factor in any factorization. The independently screened two-sided ring-unit meaning supplies the unit prerequisite.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/034O","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AUnit_of_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_algebra_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch05.irreducible-element.to.integral-domain","from_formulation":"form.05531f966e3a206f.irreducible-element","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.integral-domain","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"integral domain","editorial_reason":"The source definition explicitly assumes an integral domain, including nonzeroness and absence of zero divisors; the new domain foundation matches that scope.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/034O","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09FE","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_algebra_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch05.valuation-ring-fraction-field.to.integral-domain","from_formulation":"form.f72779e263dd3e51.valuation-ring-fraction-field","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.integral-domain","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"integral domain","editorial_reason":"A valuation ring in this source is first an integral domain with fraction field; the valuation alternative for x or x inverse is additional structure.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00I8","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09FE","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_algebra_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch05.geometrically-integral-algebra.to.integral-domain","from_formulation":"form.831db4eab7a244a4.geometrically-integral-algebra","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.integral-domain","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"integral domain","editorial_reason":"Geometric integrality requires every relevant scalar-extension algebra to be an integral domain. The independently screened nonzero commutative domain is a precise requisite, not merely a reduced ring.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05DW","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09FE","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_algebra_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch05.local-ring-unique-maximal.to.commutative-maximal-ideal","from_formulation":"form.4f7edb6d32334f67.local-ring-unique-maximal","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.commutative-maximal-ideal","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"maximal ideal","editorial_reason":"A local commutative ring in the unique-maximal-ideal formulation presupposes the maximal ideal notion, which now has a separately screened properness and inclusion definition.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07BH","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AMaximal_Ideal_of_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_algebra_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch05.local-ring-nonunit-ideal.to.unit-of-ring","from_formulation":"form.4f7edb6d32334f67.local-ring-nonunit-ideal","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.unit-of-ring","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"nonunits","editorial_reason":"The alternative local-ring criterion uses the set of elements that are not units in a commutative unital ring. The ring-unit definition provides exactly the missing notion.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07BH","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AUnit_of_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_algebra_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch05.jacobson-ring.to.commutative-maximal-ideal","from_formulation":"form.03b55b8ca41abb20.jacobson-ring","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.commutative-maximal-ideal","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"maximal ideals","editorial_reason":"The Jacobson-ring characterization quantifies over maximal ideals containing a radical ideal; maximal proper ideals in commutative unital rings are now independently defined.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00FZ","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AMaximal_Ideal_of_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_algebra_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch05.jacobson-radical-ring.to.commutative-maximal-ideal","from_formulation":"form.90b0d1ecfa394230.jacobson-radical-ring","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.commutative-maximal-ideal","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"maximal ideals","editorial_reason":"The Jacobson radical is the intersection of all maximal ideals, so the new proper maximal-ideal meaning is a direct prerequisite, independently of any extra radical properties.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AMD","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AMaximal_Ideal_of_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_algebra_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch05.jacobson-radical-unit-test.to.unit-of-ring","from_formulation":"form.90b0d1ecfa394230.jacobson-radical-unit-test","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.unit-of-ring","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"invertible","editorial_reason":"The equivalent Jacobson-radical membership criterion requires certain elements 1−xy to be units, i.e. multiplicatively invertible elements of the unital ring.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AMD","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AUnit_of_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_algebra_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
